  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   Central idea: The Erd\u0151s discrepancy problem's minimum overlap constant C is tightly constrained by the sharp peaks in the autocorrelation of step functions. The key insight is that a good minimizer should exhibit fractal-like structure or self-similarity across multiple scales. We leverage this by using sparse representations (Fourier and Haar wavelets) to find a candidate optimized over frequency and wavelet coefficients.\n\n   Why this approach might improve results:\n   - Fourier transforms decompose functions into frequency components, isolating high-discrepancy phenomena\n   - Wavelet coefficients capture local regularity and singularities, which maximize correlations\n   - The combinatorial search is driven by the merit of high-frequency content combined with sparse wavelet representation\n   - The dual representation (time+frequency+wavelets) circumvents local optima found by single-parameter searches\n\n   Bottlenecks addressed:\n   1. Non-convex landscape: Uses gradient-based optimization with adaptive step size\n   2. Noisy correlation: Pure Fourier/wavelet measurements clean up artifacts from local methods\n   3. Efficiency vs accuracy: Uses an analytical merit function rather than Monte Carlo for evaluation\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   Representation:\n     h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for x \u2208 [0,2] with \u222bh(x)dx=1\n     Discretized as N equidistant samples with dx=2/N\n\n   Variables:\n     - Fourier coefficients: a_k, b_k \u2208 [-\u03c0,\u03c0] (Haar scaling is complex but we keep it for completeness)\n\n   Exact objective:\n     C5 = (2/N) * max_{k\u2208Z} [\u222b_0^{2-k} h(x)h(x+k)dx]\n\n   However, we use:\n     F(h) = max_{k\u2208[-N+1,N-1]} [ (1/N) * CC(h, 1-h)[k] ]\n\n   Where CC is the circular cross-correlation of length N (mode='full').\n\n   Constraints:\n     0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n     \u2211h_i = N/2\n\n   Algorithmic formulation (using ADAM optimizer):\n     - Variables: vector z \u2208 R^{D} (parameters: mean, variance, frequency/scale/shear factors)\n     - Objective: sparse_reconstruction_loss(z) + \u03bb1*l1_reg(z) + \u03bb2*l2_reg(z)\n     - Satisfaction: use sigmoid(\u03c3\u00b7z + \u03b3) to map outputs to [0,1] with sum constraint\n\n   Where D is dimension (both Fourier coefficients and wavelet coefficients). Optimization uses:\n     minimize(y) = sparse_loss(measurements(z), y)\n     subject to y \u2208 [0,1]^N with \u2211y/N = 1\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   Step 1: Initial population (3 individuals)\n     a) Original parent (640 points) upsampled to N_max points\n     b) Binary representation at N_max points (thresholded to sum=N_max/2)\n     c) Sparse wavelet initialization (W denoised from Haar transform)\n\n   Step 2: Optimization loop until termination\n     a) Calculate cycle penalty from: F(z) + \u03b7\u2207F(z) (analytical gradient from Fourier/wavelets)\n     b) Update parameters via ADAM:\n        z_t = \u03bc_z/(1-\u03b21^t) + \u221a((1-\u03b22^t)/(1-\u03b22^t)) \u2207F(z_t) + \u03bb(F(z_t) - F(z_opt))\n     c) Projection to feasible set:\n        h = sigmoid(\u03c3\u00b7params) * scale_factor\n        Adjust sum(h) = N/2 via least-squares\n        ((\u03c3\u00b7params)->sigmoid)->sum adjustment: weighted array interpolation to meet constraint\n\n   Step 3: Early stopping conditions\n     - Log-probability threshold (1e-5) for hypothesis improvement\n     - Effective sample size (50% improvement threshold)\n     - Timeout condition\n\n   Step 4: Gradient computation\n     F(h) involves:\n        1. Input h to wavelet transform and Fourier transform\n        2. Compute autocorrelation (mode='full')\n        3. Record maximum value\n        4. Calculate gradient via:\n           \u2207hC5[i] = (F(h+e_i) - F(h)) / \u03b5 - (F(h) - F(h-e_i)) / \u03b5\n\n4. Search choices and variations \n   Primary search space dimensions (default):\n     - N_max = 2560 (3 levels)\n     - \u03c3_shear \u2208 {1, 0.95, 0.9}\n     - \u03bb1 \u2208 [0, 1000], \u03bb2 \u2208 [0, 1000]\n     - Gradient step size = 0.2 (tuned by second-order gradient analysis)\n     - Wavelet sub-banks: Haar, Daubechies\n\n   Adaptive triggers:\n     - Phase transitions in correlation (85% curve shape stability)\n     - Signal-to-noise ratio below 15 dB\n     - Gradual increase in \"burstiness\" of solutions\n\n   Varying optimizers (based on signal properties):\n     - Stochastic gradient descent (high-frequency processing)\n     - RMSProp (for smooth oscillatory patterns)\n     - ADAM (default with exponential moving averages)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   Time allocation:\n     - Initial parent: 5% (65s)\n     - Promising individuals: 40% (520s)\n     - Final verification: 15% (195s)\n\n   Scaling: O(N^{1.5}) per evaluation due to discrete convolutions\n\n   Failure conditions:\n     - NaN values in correlation = scale gradient step to log-scale\n     - Sum constraint fails \u2192 restart with weighted initialization\n     - Double-check in gradient calculation \u2192 5 most frequent error in 80% of trials: use finite differences only then non-analytical\n\n   Fallback structure:\n     Candidate hierarchy: 3rd party, then tried methods-most-recent-validated\n\n6. Validation and return contract \n   Pre-checks:\n     0. Feasibility test: 0\u2264h_i\u22641 and sum(h)=N/2 (high precision)\n     1. Check correlation for all lags (full scan of [-N+1,N-1])\n     2. Measure direct similarity: C5_calculated = (2/N) * corr_max\n     3. Fallback: if any test fails, use nearest neighbor in solution space\n\n   Final metrics:\n     - C5_saved (delta from 0.380936)\n     - CPU seconds spent\n     - Search resolution used (N_max)\n\n   Includes: 1-step forward checking and control flow to repair commonly occurring transient issues.",
